  "content": {
    "id": "p33-q6-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "6",
    "prompt": "Let $f:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ and $g(x)=|f(x)|$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p33-q6-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Suppose $f$ is an even function or an odd function. Prove that $g$ is an even function.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$g(-x)=|f(-x)|=|\\pm f(x)|=|f(x)|=g(x)$, so $g$ is even.",
          "worked": "Since $f$ is even or odd, $f(-x)=\\pm f(x)$. For every $x\\in\\mathbb R$,\n$$\\begin{align*}g(-x)&=|f(-x)|\\\\g(-x)&=|\\pm f(x)|\\\\g(-x)&=|f(x)|\\\\g(-x)&=g(x).\\end{align*}$$\nHence $g$ is even.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 35
      },
      {
        "id": "p33-q6-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Suppose that $g$ is an even function. Does this imply that $f$ must be an even function or an odd function? Give a proof or counterexample.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "No. A counterexample is $f(x)=\\begin{cases}1&x\\ne3,\\\\-1&x=3.\\end{cases}$\n\nThen $g(x)=1$ is even, while $f(-3)\\ne f(3)$ and $f(0)\\ne0$, so $f$ is neither even nor odd.",
          "worked": "No. Let\n$$f(x)=\\begin{cases}1&\\text{if }x\\ne3,\\\\-1&\\text{if }x=3.\\end{cases}$$\nFor every $x\\in\\mathbb R$, $g(x)=|f(x)|=1$, so $g$ is even.\nSince $f(-3)=1$ and $f(3)=-1$, $f$ is not even. Since $f(0)=1\\ne0$, $f$ is not odd. Thus an even $g$ does not require $f$ to be even or odd.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 45
      }
    ]
  },
